WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:04.237
-30 years ago, our first guests
introduced us to Bill and Ted.

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:06.372
Gosh, has it been 30 years?

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.674
Who were trying to pass
their history class

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:10.677
and write a song
that would save the world.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:12.878
Now they've reunited
for a third film

00:00:12.878 --> 00:00:15.248
called
"Bill &amp; Ted Face the Music,"

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:18.351
which is currently available
on demand and on Blu-ray.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:21.287
Here are Keanu Reeves
and Alex Winter.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:22.855
Yeah!

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:25.091
Welcome to the show.
Come on.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.293
This is so cool
to have you both here.

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.362
This is awesome.
-Thank you, sir.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:33.532
-People have been waiting
for this movie for so long.

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:36.402
First, it was "Bill and Ted's
Excellent Adventure,"

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:38.971
then "Bogus Journey,"
"Bill and Ted's Bogus Journey,"

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:40.840
which ended with them
having to write

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.442
the most important song
in history.

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:45.979
Where do this one pick up?

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:48.414
-Well, we haven't written
the song yet, Jimmy.

00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:49.715
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:52.651
-Yeah, we kind of blew it.
-Yeah, okay. Yeah.

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:56.322
-So it's like all those years
later, decades later,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.457
they're still trying to write
the song,

00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:00.392
but they haven't written
the song yet.

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:03.329
And that's put some pressures
on their wives,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:06.298
the family, the daughters.

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:08.200
And then at the same time,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.536
the future comes back

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:12.972
and has a little
wom-dinger for us.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:15.307
-Yeah, they're not happy with us
in the future anymore.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:17.510
We've lost our cred.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.512
-Wow.
-And so they've asked us --

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:23.716
They've said, like, now it's
you have to write the song

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.885
in order to save the world,

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:28.220
and all of reality
as we know it.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.590
[ Laughter ]
By a certain amount --

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.693
By a certain moment
in a certain location.

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:36.495
-Yeah. Keanu's gotten really
good at that Zoom closeup,

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.597
by the way.
-Yeah, I can't even do that.

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:41.668
Oh, it's fantastic.
You've nailed it.

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:44.636
I thought it was so fun
and so cool.

00:01:44.636 --> 00:01:46.672
Kristen Schaal was great.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.041
How did it feel...
-Thank you.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.775
...for you guys to get
into the characters again?

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:52.111
Do you remember the day that you
were officially like,

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:54.280
"We've got to do
the characters?"

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.815
I think I'm kind of
getting into it now.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.549
-Kind of, yeah.
I mean, it took a long time

00:01:57.549 --> 00:01:59.051
to get the film made.

00:01:59.051 --> 00:02:00.820
Chris Matheson and Ed Solomon
wrote a draft,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.388
we worked on it for years,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.223
worked on getting
financing together for years,

00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:07.927
bringing Dean Parisot,
who's so amazing, on to direct

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.562
and Scott Kroopf who produced.
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.830
-It was a long process.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.333
And in that process, rehearsing
and doing script work...

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:15.635
-Fitting the puzzle pieces
together.

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:18.204
-And never did we actually do
the voices.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.340
-Yeah, we wouldn't do
the voices.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.509
We would work on the script.

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:24.410
And so we had
some script readings,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.345
and Dean the director
would be like, "So..."

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:27.547
We'd be like, "No."

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:28.847
-The writers would be
looking at us like,

00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:30.049
"Are they going to do it?

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.317
Are they gonna actually
do the thing?"

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:32.819
-"Are they gonna do it?
They gonna --" Yeah.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.754
-So Keanu came over to
my house in New Orleans,

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:37.523
like pretty much the night
before We started shooting.

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:39.158
And we were rehearsing,
which we would do,

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.927
and he looked at me as
he was about to leave.

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:44.230
I was like, "What?"
He's like,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.665
"You want to just try
the characters,

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:50.236
you know, before we, like, shoot
at 6:00 in the morning?

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.105
-It's just great.
I just was so happy

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.206
to see you guys
come back together.

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:57.243
Did you guys meet auditioning
for this movie

00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.413
or were you friends
before this film?

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.481
-No, we met on the first --

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:05.051
earlier auditions we met
for the first time

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:08.688
and became friends
pretty instantaneously.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.389
I mean, we both rode
motorcycles,

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:12.658
so we both walked in
with motorcycle helmets.

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:15.428
And the auditions went on
for months and months

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.363
and they kept pairing us off
with different people

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:18.797
and changing our roles.

00:03:18.797 --> 00:03:20.499
And we became friends
over the arduous

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:22.434
"Bill &amp; Ted" audition gauntlet

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:24.636
without assuming we were
actually going to be cast.

00:03:24.636 --> 00:03:26.105
-Wow!
-And they did something

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:29.842
very unusual, where they had
different actors, artists,

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:32.344
all audition--
like ten people,

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:34.213
and like two would go
into a room

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:35.748
and then two would come out.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.517
And then another two would go
in, and then one would come out.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.620
And we were both playing
Bill and Ted,

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.287
and it was a kind of like --

00:03:42.287 --> 00:03:45.758
it was like an audition
gladiatorial gauntlet

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:47.860
of Bill and Ted.
-Torture event, yes.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.263
-I always disliked auditioning.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.931
I thought it was the worst
part of the whole business.

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:55.501
It's so kind of --
-Yeah.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.603
-They just --
-There's a fun to it, too.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:59.539
Like, Keanu and I
actually had fun

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:02.909
in the auditions playing
the characters.

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.744
It was funny, because we --
A lot of time has gone by.

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:07.279
We'd forgotten about
the early auditions

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:09.716
and the specifics of them.

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:11.617
And we thought we brought all
this stuff to our characters

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:13.719
way later once we'd had time
to really think about it

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.756
on a deep level, and then
someone showed us our audition,

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:19.958
and we literally walked in
on day one as Bill and Ted,

00:04:19.958 --> 00:04:22.361
like, just, like, no method.
It's like --

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:23.696
-You just nailed it.

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.297
-I've got my shirt
tied around my waist, like --

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:27.166
-I got shorts.
-He's got shorts and a jacket.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.634
We're like ripping --
it's like the whole thing

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.703
is literally there
on day one.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.471
-It was meant to be.

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:34.273
-There you go with that Zoom,
you nailed it.

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:36.241
-I'm not even gonna try to
compete with that closeup.

00:04:36.241 --> 00:04:38.177
It's so good.
-It's that confidence of youth,

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.711
but I love it.

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:41.514
Obviously it exploded,

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:45.017
became part of not just --
I mean, just making a movie

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:46.352
is so hard enough.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:48.754
But when the movie is a hit,
that's another thing.

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:51.023
But then when it becomes
part of pop culture

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:53.226
and people start saying
"Be excellent"

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.160
and then doing air guitar
and saying, like,

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.162
"San Dimas High School
Football rules!" and like --

00:04:57.162 --> 00:04:58.664
You must have been --
-The best --

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.533
-I know. Do people --
I mean, you're probably like,

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.534
"Don't say that again," and
people are yelling it to you,

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:04.270
and you're like --
oh, my gosh.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.106
But then you became toys,
action figures.

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:10.542
You were a cartoon,
you were a video game.

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:13.779
It was pop culture at its most.

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:16.548
What was the craziest Bill
and Ted thing that you saw?

00:05:16.548 --> 00:05:19.318
Could it have been...

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:22.221
the breakfast cereal?
-Ah!

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.756
-I've got PTSD now.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:27.593
-What, you've got a cereal?

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.295
Where did you get that?

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:30.729
-Yeah.
-Wow.

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:33.365
-Cinnamon oat squares
and marshmallow notes.

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:34.767
It sounds pretty good actually.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.769
-Yeah, please don't eat that.
Please. We care about you.

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:38.271
-It's vintage, yeah,
I will not eat this.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:39.439
-It's not.
-But how cool is that?

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:41.006
You were a breakfast cereal.

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:44.210
-Yeah, I lived in a weird part
of L.A. foe a while,

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:45.745
and I remember walking
to the grocery store

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:47.914
at like 1:00 in the morning
to get eggs or something

00:05:47.914 --> 00:05:50.149
and seeing both our faces
on a cereal --

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:51.617
'Cause they never told us
anything.

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.119
Like, they never said,
"Oh, on Thursday,

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:55.021
a cereal will come out."
They didn't pay us, either.

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:56.622
But they didn't tell us
any of that stuff,

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.292
so you would find out about it
when everyone else did.

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:00.792
-And so you walked into
your deli --

00:06:00.792 --> 00:06:02.595
-So you walked into the store
and there in front of you

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:04.464
was a Bill and Ted cereal?
-Yeah.

00:06:04.464 --> 00:06:05.898
And it was like 1:00
in the morning.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.633
-Whomp, whomp, whomp.
-I thought I was, like,

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.301
you know, having flashbacks.
-In the Twilight Zone?

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:11.203
Yeah, Rod Serling's gonna
come out and be like,

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:14.406
"Could you imagine?
Space and time..."

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:16.809
I mean, that is so cool, man.

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:19.779
I have to ask because
I started with comedy,

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:24.783
so George Carlin -- what was it
like working with him?

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:28.287
Was it -- did you -- were you
a fan of his going in?

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:31.090
Obviously you probably were.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.059
-Look how he's looking at us.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:36.162
-He's like --
-Deep respect and admiration.

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:38.530
-Deep respect and admiration.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.800
-He's like, "I'm eating lunch in
my trailer by myself today."

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.803
-Oh, God.
-He was -- I mean,

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:45.638
for both of us
it was such an honor.

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:48.307
I mean, we just were
such huge fans of his

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:51.310
and familiar with his work
and...

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.846
And though he gave us that look,
he was acting.

00:06:54.846 --> 00:06:57.083
-Yeah.
-And...

00:06:57.083 --> 00:06:58.851
-He was really nice to us,
right?

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:00.719
-He was.
He was a much gentler person

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:02.821
than you would expect
from the kind of razor --

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:04.957
the barbed nature
of his standup,

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:07.793
which was, you know, coming up
in our era, was it, right?

00:07:07.793 --> 00:07:09.695
He was a cultural titan.
-Yeah.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:12.064
-So we were pretty starstruck,
I think,

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.600
and grateful that he was
as sweet to us as he was.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.402
And, you know,
he was a gentle guy.

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:19.538
We would bump into him
even after the second movie

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:21.073
out and about.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.708
He was always very lovely.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:24.843
-Yeah, I love it when those
casts come together

00:07:24.843 --> 00:07:26.679
and it's like, there's
no one else that could do it

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.147
besides you three.

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:29.816
-No, amazing.
-It just worked out.

